      <h1 class="mt-2 max-w-4xl text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Peristaltic Pump Tubing: The Complete Guide to Materials, Sizes and Selection</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-300">By Ray Chan · August 25, 2026 · ~18 min read</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="prose prose-slate mx-auto max-w-none px-4 py-12 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p>Tubing is the only part of a peristaltic pump that touches your fluid — which makes it the most important component in the system. It is also the component that wears out fastest, which is why pump tubing is a recurring purchase for every peristaltic pump owner. Choosing the right tube is not about picking the most expensive material; it is about matching elastic recovery, chemical compatibility, temperature range, hardness and tube size to your specific application.</p>

    <p>In this guide we cover everything a buyer or engineer needs: how peristaltic pump tubing works, the four main material families, the standard size system (13# to 25#), hardness and how it affects life, the pressure and temperature limits, and a 6-step selection process you can apply to any fluid transfer job. We close with maintenance practices that extend tube life and the exact signals that tell you it is time to replace the tube.</p>

    <h2>How Peristaltic Pump Tubing Works</h2>
    <p>A peristaltic pump moves fluid by squeezing a tube with rollers or shoes. The tube sits inside a pump head between a rotating rotor and a fixed track. As the rotor turns, each roller compresses the tube against the track, trapping a pocket of fluid and pushing it forward. When the roller passes, the tube's elastic recovery creates a vacuum that pulls in the next pocket of fluid.</p>
    <p>This mechanism has three consequences that define how you choose tubing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Elastic recovery is everything.</strong> The tube must snap back to its full round shape quickly after each roller pass. Slow recovery means weak suction, pulsating flow and reduced flow rate. This is why rigid materials like standard PTFE do not work in a normal pump head.</li>
      <li><strong>The tube is a wear part.</strong> Every compression cycle flexes the tube material. Over time the tube fatigues, hardens, cracks or splits. Tube life is measured in hours of pumping, not months on the shelf.</li>
      <li><strong>Only the tube touches the fluid.</strong> The pump head, rotor and housing never contact your liquid. This is the core hygiene advantage of peristaltic pumps — and it means your tubing choice IS your chemical compatibility decision.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The Four Tubing Material Families</h2>
    <p>Peristaltic pump tubing is made from four main families of materials. Each has a clear performance profile, and the right choice depends on your fluid chemistry and temperature.</p>

    <h3>1. Silicone Tubing (Platinum-Cured)</h3>
    <p>Platinum-cured silicone is the default choice for most peristaltic pump applications. It has outstanding elastic recovery — the tube springs back almost instantly after each roller pass, which keeps flow stable and extends mechanical life. It is transparent, so you can see bubbles, particles and colour changes in the fluid. It is affordable and widely available in every tube size.</p>
    <p>Silicone handles water-based fluids, mild acids and bases, food products, pharmaceutical liquids and most biological media. Typical temperature range is −40°C to 200°C depending on formulation, though continuous high-temperature service shortens life. Silicone is not suitable for strong solvents, hydrocarbons, oils or aggressive chemicals — these cause swelling, hardening and premature failure.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> water, food and beverage, pharmaceutical, biological, laboratory media, mild chemicals.</p>

    <h3>2. PTFE-Lined Composite Tubing</h3>
    <p>Standard rigid PTFE cannot be used in a peristaltic pump head because it does not recover elastically. The solution is PTFE-lined composite tubing: a flexible outer layer (usually rubber or a reinforced polymer) that provides elastic recovery, with a thin PTFE inner liner that provides chemical resistance. The liner protects the tube wall from aggressive chemicals while the outer layer does the mechanical work.</p>
    <p>PTFE-lined tubing resists strong acids (including 98% sulfuric acid), strong bases (40% NaOH), aromatic solvents and halogenated chemicals that would destroy silicone in hours. The trade-off is higher cost and slightly lower flexibility than pure silicone.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> chemical dosing, acid and alkali transfer, aggressive solvents, industrial water treatment.</p>

    <h3>3. Norprene / Thermoplastic Elastomer Tubing</h3>
    <p>Norprene is a family of thermoplastic elastomers engineered for good chemical resistance and long flex life. It handles a wider range of chemicals than silicone — including many oils and mild solvents — while keeping good elastic recovery. It is opaque (usually black or tan), so visual inspection is limited. Norprene is a strong choice for industrial applications that are too aggressive for silicone but do not require full PTFE-level resistance.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> industrial fluids, mild solvents, oils, abrasives in suspension, general chemical transfer.</p>

    <h3>4. Specialty Tubing (Tygon, PharMed, Viton, EPDM)</h3>
    <p>Several specialty brands fill specific niches. Tygon offers a wide chemical range with moderate flex life. PharMed is a USP Class VI silicone alternative for biopharma use. Viton (FKM) handles high temperatures and aggressive chemicals but has shorter flex life. EPDM resists ozone, weather and many chemicals. These are worth considering when an application falls outside silicone/PTFE/Norprene territory, but they typically cost more per metre.</p>
    <p><strong>Best for:</strong> biopharma (PharMed), high temperature (Viton), specific chemical compatibility (Tygon, EPDM).</p>

    <h2>Tubing Size System: 13# to 25#</h2>
    <p>Peristaltic pump tubing uses a standard size numbering system based on the tubing's inner diameter (ID) and outer diameter (OD) in inches. The most common sizes are 13#, 14#, 16#, 19#, 25# and 17#, 18# for larger flows. Each pump head model specifies which tube sizes it accepts — the head's occlusion geometry and roller spacing are designed around specific tube diameters.</p>

