    <p><strong>Critical rule:</strong> always match the tube size to your pump head's specification. Using a tube with the wrong outer diameter changes the occlusion setting — too much compression shortens tube life dramatically, too little compression reduces flow and pressure capability. If your head accepts multiple sizes (for example a YZ15 head accepts 13#, 14#, 19#, 16#, 25#), the tube size determines the maximum flow rate and the minimum dose volume.</p>

    <h2>Hardness (Shore A) and Tube Life</h2>
    <p>Tube hardness, measured in Shore A, is one of the least understood but most important specifications. Softer tubing (50–60 Shore A) recovers quickly and seals well at low pressure, but it wears out faster under repeated compression and cannot handle high system pressure. Harder tubing (65–80 Shore A) lasts longer mechanically and handles higher pressure, but it may not recover fully at low speeds or in cold conditions, which reduces flow consistency.</p>
    <p>Manufacturers rate each tube material for a recommended hardness range. Silicone is typically 50–70 Shore A depending on the formulation; Norprene and specialty tubes are often 60–75 Shore A. When a pump head has an adjustable occlusion setting, you can tune the roller pressure to the tube hardness — a small adjustment that significantly extends tube life.</p>

    <h2>Pressure and Temperature Limits</h2>
    <p>Standard peristaltic pump tubing is designed for discharge pressures up to about 1 bar (14.5 psi) in continuous service. Heavy-wall reinforced tubing can operate at higher pressures — some industrial heads reach 3 to 6 bar with the correct tube. Exceeding the pressure rating causes the tube to balloon, reducing compression efficiency and accelerating wear; at high pressure the tube can fail suddenly.</p>
    <p>Temperature limits are set by the tube material, not the pump. Silicone handles roughly −40°C to 200°C in intermittent service, with continuous use best kept below about 150°C. PTFE-lined tubing has a similar mechanical limit from its outer layer, though the liner itself withstands far higher temperatures. Always check both the fluid temperature at the pump and the ambient temperature — a cold environment makes tubing stiffer and slows recovery.</p>

    <h2>How to Select Peristaltic Pump Tubing: 6-Step Process</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>List your fluid properties.</strong> Write down the liquid, its concentration, pH, viscosity, temperature and whether it contains solids. This determines the material family: water-based and food → silicone; strong acids/bases/solvents → PTFE-lined; oils and mild chemicals → Norprene.</li>
      <li><strong>Define flow rate and pressure.</strong> Your required flow rate (ml/min or L/min) and discharge pressure (bar) decide the tube size and whether you need heavy-wall tubing. Use the pump head's flow matrix to pick the tube size that reaches your target flow at a mid-range speed.</li>
      <li><strong>Check chemical compatibility.</strong> Verify the tube material against your specific chemical. A compatibility chart lists resistance at the fluid's concentration and temperature. When in doubt, request a sample and run a 48-hour soak test — swelling, hardening or discolouration means the material is not suitable.</li>
      <li><strong>Match the tube to the pump head.</strong> Confirm the tube's outer diameter and wall thickness match the head's specification. Never improvise a tube size; it changes occlusion and voids performance guarantees.</li>
      <li><strong>Choose the right hardness.</strong> For low-pressure precision dosing, a softer tube gives better low-flow consistency. For high-pressure or continuous industrial service, choose a harder tube with heavy wall. If your head has adjustable occlusion, set it to the tube's recommended compression.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan the consumable budget.</strong> Tube life is measured in hours; buy spares in multiples of your pump's tube count (a 2-channel head needs 2 tubes). Keep a spare set in stock — tube failure is the most common cause of unplanned peristaltic pump downtime.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Common Mistakes in Tubing Selection</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Choosing by chemical resistance alone.</strong> A tube that resists the chemical but recovers slowly will deliver poor flow and wear the pump head. Elastic recovery matters as much as chemistry.</li>
      <li><strong>Using rigid PTFE in a standard head.</strong> Standard PTFE tube will not recover after roller compression — you need PTFE-lined composite tubing, not bare PTFE.</li>
      <li><strong>Ignoring pressure.</strong> High system pressure shortens tube life far faster than chemistry. If your application runs above 1 bar, use heavy-wall tubing and check the head's pressure rating.</li>
      <li><strong>Wrong hardness.</strong> Too-soft tubing fails early; too-hard tubing gives pulsating flow. Match hardness to duty cycle, not to price.</li>
      <li><strong>Not tracking tube hours.</strong> Tubes fail without warning if you do not log running hours. Replace on a schedule based on the material's rated life, not on visual appearance alone.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Maintenance That Extends Tube Life</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Run at the lowest speed that meets your flow.</strong> Slower roller speed means fewer compression cycles per litre pumped, which extends tube life.</li>
      <li><strong>Release occlusion when idle.</strong> If the pump sits unused for long periods, loosen the head or remove the tube so it is not permanently compressed in one spot.</li>
      <li><strong>Flush after aggressive fluids.</strong> After pumping chemicals, flush the line with a compatible rinse fluid to remove residue that can harden the tube wall.</li>
      <li><strong>Keep spares in stock.</strong> Have a replacement tube ready; swap immediately when flow drops by 10% or visible cracks appear.</li>
      <li><strong>Log hours.</strong> A simple running-hours log lets you replace tubes on schedule before they fail mid-batch.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Replacement Signals: When to Change the Tube</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Flow rate drops by 10% or more</strong> at the same speed — the tube has lost elasticity or is partially blocked.</li>
      <li><strong>Visible cracks, splits or whitening</strong> on the tube wall.</li>
      <li><strong>Pulsating or surging flow</strong> that was previously smooth.</li>
      <li><strong>Discolouration or swelling</strong> — a sign of chemical incompatibility, not normal wear.</li>
      <li><strong>Droplets or wetness</strong> on the pump head — the tube has failed and is leaking.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Buying Peristaltic Pump Tubing: What to Ask the Supplier</h2>
    <p>When you buy tubing from a manufacturer, ask for these five specifications in writing: (1) exact material formulation and Shore A hardness, (2) ID/OD/wall in millimetres, (3) rated pressure at continuous service, (4) temperature range and any continuous-use limit, and (5) expected flex life in hours at your operating speed. A manufacturer that publishes full spec tables is far more reliable than one selling "high-quality tubing" without numbers. For OEM volume, ask about custom lengths, custom hardness formulations and PTFE-lined options — these are standard capabilities at established peristaltic pump manufacturers.</p>

    <h2>Conclusion</h2>
    <p>Peristaltic pump tubing is a consumable with a big impact: the right tube keeps flow accurate, extends pump life and prevents downtime; the wrong tube fails early and can contaminate your process. Start with your fluid chemistry, match the material family (silicone / PTFE-lined / Norprene / specialty), confirm the size against your pump head, choose the hardness for your duty cycle, and log hours so you replace tubes before they fail. If you are unsure which tube fits your application, send us your fluid details — liquid, temperature, pressure and flow rate — and we will recommend a matched tube and head combination.</p>

